SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);

INSERT INTO mmd_blog_post
  (title, url_key, excerpt, content, author, status, published_at, related_skus, source_sku,
   meta_title, meta_description, meta_keywords, likes, linkedin_urn, facebook_post_id,
   created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'Enhance Work Productivity with Microsoft 365 Copilot: A Practical Guide for Singapore Teams', 'microsoft-365-copilot-work-productivity-guide', CONCAT('Microsoft 365 Copilot is the one AI assistant that already knows your emails, meetings and SharePoint documents ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' here is what it does in Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, Outlook and Loop, the prompt structure that gets reliable output, a one-week team rollout, and the WSQ course that teaches it hands-on with up to 70% funding.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> Microsoft 365 Copilot is not another chatbot tab. It is the same generative AI you have used in a browser, wired into Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, Outlook and Loop, and <em>grounded</em> in your organisation&rsquo;s own data &mdash; the emails, meetings, chats and SharePoint documents you already have permission to see. That grounding is the difference between a clever writing toy and an assistant that can summarise last week&rsquo;s project thread, draft the reply, build the deck from the spec on SharePoint and analyse the sales workbook without you copying anything anywhere. This guide covers what Copilot does in each app, the prompt structure that produces reliable output, a one-week rollout plan for a team, and how to go one step further with a Copilot Studio agent. If you want to learn it hands-on and funded, the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhance-work-productivity-with-microsoft-365-copilot.html">WSQ Enhance Work Productivity with Microsoft 365 Copilot</a> course (TGS-2024043856) covers all of it in two days with up to 70% WSQ funding.</p> <h2>Why Copilot is different from the chatbot in your browser</h2> <p>Most people met generative AI through a public chatbot. You paste text in, you get text out, and the model knows nothing about your company. That is fine for a first draft of a birthday message. It is useless for the question your manager actually asks at 9:05am: <em>&ldquo;What did the vendor say about the delivery delay, and what have we already promised the client?&rdquo;</em></p> <p>Microsoft 365 Copilot answers that question because it sits on top of <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/microsoft-365/microsoft-365-copilot-overview">Microsoft Graph</a> &mdash; the index of your organisation&rsquo;s mail, calendar, Teams chats, meeting transcripts and SharePoint and OneDrive files. When you ask Copilot something, it retrieves the relevant items you are allowed to see, adds them to the prompt as context, and only then calls the large language model. Microsoft calls this <em>grounding</em>, and it is the single most important idea in this article. Three consequences follow from it:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Answers cite your documents, not the internet.</strong> Copilot shows the files and messages it used, so you can check the source instead of trusting a paragraph.</li> <li><strong>Permissions are respected.</strong> According to <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/microsoft-365/microsoft-365-copilot-privacy">Microsoft&rsquo;s documentation</a>, Copilot only surfaces content the signed-in user already has access to. A junior analyst asking about &ldquo;the restructuring plan&rdquo; gets nothing that was not already shared with them.</li> <li><strong>Your prompts and data stay inside your tenant.</strong> The same documentation states that prompts, responses and Graph data are not used to train the foundation models. This is the point that usually unlocks a nervous IT department.</li> </ul> <p>The practical implication for a Singapore team: the quality of Copilot&rsquo;s answers is largely the quality of what is on SharePoint. Tidy document libraries, clear file names and a sensible folder structure suddenly pay a direct productivity dividend. Teams that still keep their real work in personal desktops and WhatsApp screenshots get a much dumber assistant.</p> <h2>Free Copilot, Copilot Pro or Microsoft 365 Copilot?</h2> <p>The naming is confusing and the course spends its first hour clearing it up, because the three products are not interchangeable.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Who it is for</th><th>Knows your work data?</th><th>Works inside Office apps?</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Copilot (free, in Windows, Edge and the Copilot app)</td><td>Individuals, general questions, drafting from scratch</td><td>No</td><td>No &mdash; you copy and paste</td></tr> <tr><td>Copilot Pro (personal subscription)</td><td>Individuals on a personal Microsoft 365 plan</td><td>No (personal OneDrive only)</td><td>Yes, in the personal Office apps</td></tr> <tr><td>Microsoft 365 Copilot (business add-on)</td><td>Organisations on Microsoft 365 business or enterprise plans</td><td><strong>Yes &mdash; grounded on Microsoft Graph and SharePoint</strong></td><td>Yes, in Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, Outlook, Loop and more</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Everything that follows assumes the third one. It is the only version that can read your meeting transcript, find the pricing sheet on SharePoint and draft the client email in one instruction.</p> <h2>What Copilot actually does in each app</h2> <p>The fastest way to understand Copilot&rsquo;s power is to see one realistic task per application. These are the exercises the course walks through, and every one of them starts from data that is already in Microsoft 365.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>App</th><th>A real task</th><th>What Copilot does</th><th>What you still check</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Word</td><td>Turn a 12-page tender specification on SharePoint into a two-page proposal outline</td><td>Reads the referenced file, drafts sections and headings in your document, rewrites tone on request</td><td>Commercial terms and anything that sounds like a promise</td></tr> <tr><td>PowerPoint</td><td>Create a first-cut deck from that Word proposal</td><td>Builds slides, suggests layouts and speaker notes, adds an agenda and summary slide</td><td>Slide order, branding, one idea per slide (it will over-stuff)</td></tr> <tr><td>Excel</td><td>Analyse a sales workbook by region and quarter</td><td>Inserts formula columns, builds pivot tables and charts, highlights trends and outliers in plain English</td><td>That the data is a proper table with headers; formulas it added</td></tr> <tr><td>Teams</td><td>Catch up on a meeting you missed</td><td>Summarises the transcript, lists decisions and action items with owners, answers follow-up questions about who said what</td><td>Decisions that were implied but not stated aloud</td></tr> <tr><td>Outlook</td><td>Reply to a 30-message thread</td><td>Summarises the thread, drafts a reply in the tone you specify, coaches the draft for clarity</td><td>Names, dates and anything with a number in it</td></tr> <tr><td>Loop</td><td>Run a project workspace</td><td>Generates and reshapes page content, keeps components live across Teams and Outlook, tracks tasks</td><td>That the right people have access to the workspace</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Two apps deserve a closer look because they are where the productivity gains are largest.</p> <h3>Excel: the analysis you kept putting off</h3> <p>Copilot in Excel works on data formatted as a table. Ask it to <em>&ldquo;add a column for gross margin as a percentage&rdquo;</em> and it writes the formula and explains it; ask it to <em>&ldquo;show total revenue by region and quarter as a pivot table with a chart&rdquo;</em> and it builds both. Microsoft&rsquo;s own <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/get-started-with-copilot-in-excel-d7110502-0334-4b4f-a175-a73abdfc118a">getting-started guide</a> is explicit that the sheet must be a table with headers, which is the number one reason first attempts fail in class. The second reason is vague asks: &ldquo;analyse this&rdquo; gets a generic summary, while &ldquo;which three customers had the biggest quarter-on-quarter drop and by how much&rdquo; gets an answer you can act on.</p> <h3>Teams and Outlook: the meetings and mail you no longer have to re-read</h3> <p>Meeting recap is the feature that converts sceptics. With transcription on, Copilot can produce the notes, the decisions and the action items before the meeting has ended, and you can ask it questions like <em>&ldquo;what did Priya say about the launch date?&rdquo;</em> afterwards. In Outlook the equivalent is thread summary plus drafting, which turns a 20-minute reply into a two-minute review. The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-microsoft-365-copilot-for-teams.html">WSQ Microsoft 365 Copilot for Teams</a> course goes deeper on the collaboration side if that is where most of your day is spent.</p> <h2>The prompt structure that produces usable output</h2> <p>Copilot does not need clever phrasing, but it does need four things that people leave out. In class we use the acronym <strong>GCSF</strong>: Goal, Context, Source, Format.</p> <ol> <li><strong>Goal</strong> &mdash; what you want and who it is for. <em>&ldquo;Draft a one-page status update for the steering committee.&rdquo;</em></li> <li><strong>Context</strong> &mdash; what Copilot cannot know. <em>&ldquo;The project is two weeks behind because of a vendor delay; tone should be candid but not alarming.&rdquo;</em></li> <li><strong>Source</strong> &mdash; the grounding. Reference the file, meeting or thread explicitly using the slash-reference in Copilot, e.g. <em>/Project Falcon weekly sync</em> or <em>/Q4 pricing sheet</em>. This is what makes the answer yours instead of generic.</li> <li><strong>Format</strong> &mdash; the shape. <em>&ldquo;Three headings: progress, risks, decisions needed. Bullet points, under 250 words.&rdquo;</em></li> </ol> <pre>Draft a one-page status update for the steering committee (Goal).&#10;The project is two weeks behind because of a vendor delay;&#10;be candid but not alarming (Context).&#10;Use /Project Falcon weekly sync and /Falcon risk register (Source).&#10;Three headings: Progress, Risks, Decisions needed.&#10;Bullet points, under 250 words (Format).</pre> <p>A prompt with all four parts gets a first draft that needs light editing. A prompt with one part gets something you rewrite from scratch, and then you conclude that Copilot &ldquo;does not work&rdquo;. The gap between those two outcomes is a habit, not a feature, which is why the course drills it in every application.</p> <h2>A one-week rollout for a small team</h2> <p>Licences do not change behaviour; a plan does. This is the sequence we recommend to a team of five to fifteen people, and it maps directly onto the course&rsquo;s final exercise of designing an integration and staff-training plan.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Focus</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Monday</td><td>Tidy SharePoint: one library per project, clear file names, retire duplicates</td><td>Copilot has something clean to ground on</td></tr> <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Turn on meeting transcription; everyone tries Teams recap on one meeting</td><td>First visible win, zero writing skill required</td></tr> <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Outlook thread summary and drafting with the GCSF prompt</td><td>Each person saves a measurable 30 minutes</td></tr> <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Word and PowerPoint from a SharePoint source document</td><td>A real deck produced in a quarter of the usual time</td></tr> <tr><td>Friday</td><td>Excel on a live workbook, then a 30-minute retro: what worked, what to standardise</td><td>A shared prompt library and two or three team conventions</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The retro matters more than any single feature. Teams that write down five prompts that worked, and agree on where files live, keep the productivity gain. Teams that skip it drift back to old habits within a month. If you would rather compress the whole week into two structured days with an instructor, that is exactly what the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhance-work-productivity-with-microsoft-365-copilot.html">WSQ Microsoft 365 Copilot course</a> is for.</p> <h2>One step further: your own agent with Copilot Studio</h2> <p>Copilot in the apps is general purpose. The moment you want it to answer the same questions the same way for everyone &mdash; &ldquo;what is our travel claim policy?&rdquo;, &ldquo;how do I request a laptop?&rdquo; &mdash; you want an <em>agent</em>. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio">Microsoft Copilot Studio</a> lets you build one without code: give it instructions, point it at a SharePoint document library as its <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-add-sharepoint">knowledge source</a>, and publish it into Teams. It answers from those documents, respects the same permissions, and hands off to a person when it cannot help.</p> <p>The course finishes with each learner building and publishing a simple agent of their own, because it is the natural bridge from &ldquo;Copilot makes me faster&rdquo; to &ldquo;Copilot makes the team faster&rdquo;. We have written a separate <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/build-chatbots-with-microsoft-copilot-studio">walkthrough of building chatbots and agents in Copilot Studio</a> if you want a preview, and the follow-on <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-business-process-automation-with-power-automate-and-copilot-studio-agents.html">WSQ Business Process Automation with Power Automate and Copilot Studio Agents</a> course takes agents all the way to automated approvals.</p> <h2>Governance in three sentences</h2> <p>Copilot inherits your existing Microsoft 365 permissions, so over-shared SharePoint sites become over-shared answers &mdash; review sharing before rollout, not after. Microsoft&rsquo;s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/microsoft-365/microsoft-365-copilot-ai-security">security guidance</a> and the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/microsoft-365/microsoft-365-copilot-architecture">architecture overview</a> describe how sensitivity labels and data loss prevention carry through to Copilot output, so use the labels you already have. And agree one rule from day one: a human reads every AI draft that leaves the organisation.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhance-work-productivity-with-microsoft-365-copilot.html">WSQ &ndash; Enhance Work Productivity with Microsoft 365 Copilot</a> course (TGS-2024043856) is a two-day, instructor-led programme delivered by an authorised Microsoft Learning Partner. Four topics cover exactly the ground above:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Overview of Microsoft 365 Copilot</strong> &mdash; free Copilot versus Copilot Pro versus Microsoft 365 Copilot, content creation, and building and improving presentations in PowerPoint.</li> <li><strong>Copilot for Excel</strong> &mdash; formula columns, charts and pivot tables, and plain-English data analysis.</li> <li><strong>Copilot for Teams and Outlook</strong> &mdash; Microsoft 365 Chat, meeting and chat summaries, working with prompts, drafting and summarising email.</li> <li><strong>Copilot for Microsoft Loop and Copilot Studio</strong> &mdash; Loop workspaces, sharing and project management, and creating your own agent in Copilot Studio.</li> </ol> <p>Assessment is a written and a practical exam, and every learner leaves with a personal plan for integrating Copilot into their workflow and a staff-training plan for their team.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$900.00 before GST (S$81.00 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$531.00</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$351.00</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The nett fee can be further offset with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> for eligible Singaporeans, PSEA for eligible younger learners, and SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC) for eligible Singapore-registered companies; employer-sponsored SMEs can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Figures are taken from the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>It is part of our wider <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/microsoft-copilot-series.html">Microsoft Copilot series</a>, which also includes <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-microsoft-365-copilot-for-teams.html">Copilot for Teams</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-copilot-for-power-automate.html">Copilot for Power Automate</a> and the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/ab-900-microsoft-365-copilot-and-agent-administration-fundamentals.html">AB-900 Copilot and agent administration</a> certification prep.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does Microsoft 365 Copilot use my company data to train the AI?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Microsoft&rsquo;s documentation states that prompts, responses and data accessed through Microsoft Graph are not used to train the foundation large language models. Your data is used only to ground the answer you asked for, inside your tenant.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can Copilot see files I do not have permission to open?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Copilot only retrieves content the signed-in user already has access to in SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams and Outlook. The flip side is that any site that is over-shared today becomes visible in Copilot answers, so review sharing settings before rollout.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need Copilot Pro or Microsoft 365 Copilot for work?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>For work data you need Microsoft 365 Copilot, the business add-on, because it is the only version grounded on your organisation&rsquo;s Microsoft Graph. Copilot Pro is a personal subscription that works in the Office apps but only with your personal files.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Why does Copilot in Excel say it cannot analyse my sheet?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Almost always because the data is not formatted as an Excel table with a single header row. Select the range, press Ctrl+T, and try again. Merged cells, blank header cells and totals rows inside the data also confuse it.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is the WSQ Microsoft 365 Copilot course eligible for SkillsFuture Credit?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. TGS-2024043856 is WSQ funded at 50% for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above, and 70% for Singaporeans aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff. Eligible Singaporeans can use SkillsFuture Credit to offset the nett fee, and companies can tap SFEC.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need a Copilot licence to attend the course?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Training accounts are provided for the hands-on exercises. You should bring a laptop; a spare can be arranged if you do not have one.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>The power of Microsoft 365 Copilot is not the model. It is that the model arrives already grounded in your own emails, meetings and SharePoint documents, inside the apps where the work happens, with your permissions intact. Learn one prompt structure, tidy the data it grounds on, and roll it out with a plan rather than a licence email, and a team gets back hours every week. Go one step further with a Copilot Studio agent and the whole organisation gets the same answer to the same question every time.</p> <p><strong>Ready to make it stick?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhance-work-productivity-with-microsoft-365-copilot.html">Register for WSQ &ndash; Enhance Work Productivity with Microsoft 365 Copilot</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
